  {1, 0, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Hydrogen-1 (H)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.53 Å  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  period 152 as<br>melts at −259 °C, boils at −253 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Hydrogen-1</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>1 proton</b> and <b>no neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 1 proton always means hydrogen. Protons plus neutrons make 1, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Hydrogen has <b>1</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">For hydrogen that is exactly right: a real hydrogen atom has one proton and one electron, so what you see is a real atom.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this atom the lowest orbit is about <b>53 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Hydrogen-1 (H) · n=1 · r 0.53 Å · E −13.61 eV · period 152 as<br>melts at −259 °C, boils at −253 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Hydrogen-1 (H)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. </li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For hydrogen-1 the nucleus is about <b>1.7 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>6.3×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>220 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 441 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>472 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 945 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.1 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.1 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 4.2 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>6.9 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 14 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>16 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.6 times a 10 km drive. The whole atom would be 31 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 1.01 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:99.94556794236551%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 99.9 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">0 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">This atom has none.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0544 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Hydrogen-1 (0.0544 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Hydrogen-1 is one proton and one electron, so there is nothing to bind.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Pick a bigger nucleus, like helium-4 or iron-56, and open this panel again.</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Hydrogen-1</b> atom weighs 1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.49×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">5.98×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">4.18×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Hydrogen-1 holds about <b>5.98×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 1.89×10¹⁶ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Hydrogen-1</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.0 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">2 up quarks and 1 down quarks, weighing 8.99 MeV/c² together, out of 938 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Hydrogen-1</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Hydrogen-1 atom (yours)</b><br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.60649635983714%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {1, 0, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", true, "Hydrogen-1 (H)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.53 Å  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  period 152 as<br>melts at −434 °F, boils at −423 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Hydrogen-1</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>1 proton</b> and <b>no neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 1 proton always means hydrogen. Protons plus neutrons make 1, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Hydrogen has <b>1</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">For hydrogen that is exactly right: a real hydrogen atom has one proton and one electron, so what you see is a real atom.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this atom the lowest orbit is about <b>53 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Hydrogen-1 (H) · n=1 · r 0.53 Å · E −13.61 eV · period 152 as<br>melts at −434 °F, boils at −423 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Hydrogen-1 (H)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. </li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For hydrogen-1 the nucleus is about <b>1.7 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>6.3×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (0.3 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>723 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 1,447 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (0.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1,550 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 3,100 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (2.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.3 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.1 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 2.6 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (8.7 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>4.3 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 8.6 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (1.6 ft across), the electron's orbit would be <b>9.8 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.6 times a 10 km drive. The whole atom would be 20 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.3×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6.3×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10¹⁴</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (0.8 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Hydrogen-1</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 1.01 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:99.94556794236551%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 99.9 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">0 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">This atom has none.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0544 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Hydrogen-1 (0.0544 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Hydrogen-1 is one proton and one electron, so there is nothing to bind.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Pick a bigger nucleus, like helium-4 or iron-56, and open this panel again.</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Hydrogen-1</b> atom weighs 1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.49×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">5.98×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">4.18×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-1 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Hydrogen-1 holds about <b>5.98×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 1.89×10¹⁶ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Hydrogen-1</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.0 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">2 up quarks and 1 down quarks, weighing 8.99 MeV/c² together, out of 938 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Hydrogen-1</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Hydrogen-1 atom (yours)</b><br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.60649635983714%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {1, 1, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Hydrogen-2 (H)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.53 Å  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  period 152 as<br>melts at −259 °C, boils at −253 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Hydrogen-2</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>1 proton</b> and <b>1 neutron</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 1 proton always means hydrogen. Protons plus neutrons make 2, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Hydrogen has <b>1</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">For hydrogen that is exactly right: a real hydrogen atom has one proton and one electron, so what you see is a real atom.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this atom the lowest orbit is about <b>53 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Hydrogen-2 (H) · n=1 · r 0.53 Å · E −13.61 eV · period 152 as<br>melts at −259 °C, boils at −253 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Hydrogen-2 (H)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. </li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For hydrogen-2 the nucleus is about <b>3.0 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>3.5×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>123 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 245 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>263 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 525 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.2 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 2.3 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3.9 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 7.7 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>8.8 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.9 times a 10 km drive. The whole atom would be 18 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 2.01 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:49.951970188896524%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:50.020825117826384%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0272 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Hydrogen-2 (0.0272 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Hydrogen-2</b> on its own (1 proton, 1 neutron, 1 electron), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>2.02 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>2.01 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>2.39×10⁻³ u</b> (0.118 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (2.22 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.11 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.2×10⁵× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:12.355555555555558%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Hydrogen-2: 1.11 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Hydrogen-2</b> atom weighs 3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.47×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.99×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.09×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Hydrogen-2 holds about <b>2.99×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 9.47×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Hydrogen-2</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">3 up quarks and 3 down quarks, weighing 20.5 MeV/c² together, out of 1.88e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Hydrogen-2</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Hydrogen-2 atom (yours)</b><br><small>3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.09070956897168%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {1, 1, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", true, "Hydrogen-2 (H)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.53 Å  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  period 152 as<br>melts at −434 °F, boils at −423 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Hydrogen-2</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>1 proton</b> and <b>1 neutron</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 1 proton always means hydrogen. Protons plus neutrons make 2, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Hydrogen has <b>1</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">For hydrogen that is exactly right: a real hydrogen atom has one proton and one electron, so what you see is a real atom.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this atom the lowest orbit is about <b>53 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Hydrogen-2 (H) · n=1 · r 0.53 Å · E −13.61 eV · period 152 as<br>melts at −434 °F, boils at −423 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Hydrogen-2 (H)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. </li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For hydrogen-2 the nucleus is about <b>3.0 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>3.5×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (0.3 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>402 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 804 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (0.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>861 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 1,722 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (2.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3,847 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 1.5 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (8.7 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.4 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 4.8 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (1.6 ft across), the electron's orbit would be <b>5.4 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.9 times a 10 km drive. The whole atom would be 11 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.5×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3.5×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.3×10¹³</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Hydrogen atom has 1 electron, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.5 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (53 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Hydrogen-2</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 2.01 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:49.951970188896524%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:50.020825117826384%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0272 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Hydrogen-2 (0.0272 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Hydrogen-2</b> on its own (1 proton, 1 neutron, 1 electron), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>2.02 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>2.01 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>2.39×10⁻³ u</b> (0.118 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (2.22 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.11 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.2×10⁵× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:12.355555555555558%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Hydrogen-2: 1.11 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Hydrogen-2</b> atom weighs 3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.47×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.99×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.09×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Hydrogen-2 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Hydrogen-2 holds about <b>2.99×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 9.47×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Hydrogen-2</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">3 up quarks and 3 down quarks, weighing 20.5 MeV/c² together, out of 1.88e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Hydrogen-2</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Hydrogen-2 atom (yours)</b><br><small>3.34×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.09070956897168%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {2, 2, "Cloud", "Modern", "2p", false, "Helium-4 (He⁺)  ·  2pz orbital  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 1.32 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>no melting point at normal pressure, boils at −269 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Helium-4</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>2 protons</b> and <b>2 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 2 protons always means helium. Protons plus neutrons make 4, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Helium has <b>2</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal helium atom. It is a helium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>He⁺</b>: a helium atom that has lost 1 of its 2 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>26 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Cloud|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the modern <b>quantum</b> picture (Erwin Schrödinger, 1926). An electron does not have an exact position or path. Instead you get a <b>probability cloud</b>: it is denser where the electron is more likely to be found.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Each dot is one place the electron could be found. Measure where an electron is, again and again, and the spots slowly build up this shape. So the cloud is a <b>map of chances</b>, not a picture of a path.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The name, like <b>2p</b>, has two parts. The number is the energy level (n). The letter is the shape: <b>s</b> is a sphere, <b>p</b> is a dumbbell, and <b>d</b> has more lobes and rings, and <b>f</b> has even more. A small letter after it, like <b>z</b> in 2p<sub>z</sub>, says which way the shape points. The two colours are the two signs of the electron’s wave: where they meet, the wave is zero and the electron is never found (a node). This is the <b>side view</b>: drag to move around.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Helium-4 (He⁺) · 2pz orbital · E −13.61 eV · avg r: sampled …, theory 1.32 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>no melting point at normal pressure, boils at −269 °C · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Helium-4 (He⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>2p orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For helium-4 the nucleus is about <b>3.8 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>1.4×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>49 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 97 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>104 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about the same as a football pitch. The whole atom would be 208 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>465 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 931 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.5 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.5 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 3.1 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3.5 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 6.9 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 4 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:49.951970188896524%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:50.020825117826384%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0272 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Helium-4 (0.0272 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Helium-4</b> on its own (2 protons, 2 neutrons, 2 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>4.03 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>4 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.0304 u</b> (0.753 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (28.3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.07 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.6%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Helium-4: 7.07 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Helium-4</b> atom weighs 6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.76×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.50×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.05×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Helium-4 holds about <b>1.50×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 4.77×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Helium-4</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">6 up quarks and 6 down quarks, weighing 41 MeV/c² together, out of 3.76e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Helium-4</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Helium-4 atom (yours)</b><br><small>6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.571000971400583%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {2, 2, "Cloud", "Modern", "2p", true, "Helium-4 (He⁺)  ·  2pz orbital  ·  E −13.61 eV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 1.32 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>no melting point at normal pressure, boils at −452 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Helium-4</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>2 protons</b> and <b>2 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 2 protons always means helium. Protons plus neutrons make 4, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Helium has <b>2</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal helium atom. It is a helium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>He⁺</b>: a helium atom that has lost 1 of its 2 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>26 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Cloud|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the modern <b>quantum</b> picture (Erwin Schrödinger, 1926). An electron does not have an exact position or path. Instead you get a <b>probability cloud</b>: it is denser where the electron is more likely to be found.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Each dot is one place the electron could be found. Measure where an electron is, again and again, and the spots slowly build up this shape. So the cloud is a <b>map of chances</b>, not a picture of a path.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The name, like <b>2p</b>, has two parts. The number is the energy level (n). The letter is the shape: <b>s</b> is a sphere, <b>p</b> is a dumbbell, and <b>d</b> has more lobes and rings, and <b>f</b> has even more. A small letter after it, like <b>z</b> in 2p<sub>z</sub>, says which way the shape points. The two colours are the two signs of the electron’s wave: where they meet, the wave is zero and the electron is never found (a node). This is the <b>side view</b>: drag to move around.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Helium-4 (He⁺) · 2pz orbital · E −13.61 eV · avg r: sampled …, theory 1.32 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>no melting point at normal pressure, boils at −452 °F · gas at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Helium-4 (He⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>2p orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For helium-4 the nucleus is about <b>3.8 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>1.4×10⁴ times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (0.3 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>159 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 319 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (0.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>342 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about the same as a football pitch. The whole atom would be 684 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (2.6 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1,527 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.4 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 3,053 ft wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (8.7 in across), the electron's orbit would be <b>5,013 ft</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.5 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 1.9 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (1.6 ft across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.2 mi</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 4.3 mi wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁴ : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 1.4×10⁴ nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10¹²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Helium atom has 2 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (26 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Helium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 4 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:49.951970188896524%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:50.020825117826384%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0272 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Helium-4 (0.0272 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Helium-4</b> on its own (2 protons, 2 neutrons, 2 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>4.03 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>4 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.0304 u</b> (0.753 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (28.3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.07 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.6%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Helium-4: 7.07 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Helium-4</b> atom weighs 6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.76×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.50×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.05×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Helium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Helium-4 holds about <b>1.50×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 4.77×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Helium-4</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">6 up quarks and 6 down quarks, weighing 41 MeV/c² together, out of 3.76e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Helium-4</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Helium-4 atom (yours)</b><br><small>6.65×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.571000971400583%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {6, 8, "Slice", "Modern", "3d", false, "Carbon-14 (C⁵⁺)  ·  3dz² orbital  ·  E −54.42 eV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 0.93 Å<br>cut plane: y = 0.00 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>sublimes at 3,627 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 5,700 years)",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Carbon-14</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>6 protons</b> and <b>8 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 6 protons always means carbon. Protons plus neutrons make 14, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>radioactive (half-life 5,700 years)</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Carbon has <b>6</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal carbon atom. It is a carbon nucleus with a single electron, called <b>C⁵⁺</b>: a carbon atom that has lost 5 of its 6 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>8.8 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Slice|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the same quantum cloud, but <b>cut in half</b>, like slicing a fruit and looking at the flat cut. The brightness shows how likely the electron is to be found at each spot on that cut.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Blue and orange are the two <b>signs</b> (+ and −) of the electron’s wave, called ψ. Where they meet, the wave passes through zero. That is called a <b>node</b>, and the electron is never found exactly there.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Drag up or down on the picture, or use the cut controls, to move the cut through the cloud. The name, like <b>3d</b>, has the energy level (n) as a number and the shape as a letter.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Carbon-14 (C⁵⁺) · 3dz² orbital · E −54.42 eV · avg r: sampled …, theory 0.93 Å<br>cut plane: y = 0.00 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>sublimes at 3,627 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 5,700 years)</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Carbon-14 (C⁵⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>3d orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li><li><b>cut plane</b>: where the knife went through the cloud.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For carbon-14 the nucleus is about <b>5.8 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>3,049 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>11 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.9 times a bus. The whole atom would be 21 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3,049 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3,049 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.8×10¹⁰</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Carbon atom has 6 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (2.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (8.8 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>23 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.9 times a bus. The whole atom would be 46 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3,049 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3,049 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.8×10¹⁰</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Carbon atom has 6 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (2.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (8.8 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>102 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about the same as a football pitch. The whole atom would be 204 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3,049 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3,049 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.8×10¹⁰</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Carbon atom has 6 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (2.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (8.8 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>335 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about the same as the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 671 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3,049 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3,049 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.8×10¹⁰</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Carbon atom has 6 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (2.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (8.8 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>762 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 1.5 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3,049 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 3,049 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.8×10¹⁰</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Carbon atom has 6 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (2.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (8.8 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Carbon-14</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>2.33×10⁻²⁶ kg</b>. That is 14 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:42.81342703430404%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:57.16325604453738%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 42.8 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">8 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 57.2 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0233 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Carbon-14 (0.0233 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Carbon-14</b> on its own (6 protons, 8 neutrons, 6 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>14.1 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>14 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.113 u</b> (0.801 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (105 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.52 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.5×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:83.55555555555556%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Carbon-14: 7.52 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Carbon-14</b> atom weighs 2.33×10⁻²⁶ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.08×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Carbon-14 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">4.30×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Carbon-14 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.01×10²⁷ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Carbon-14 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Carbon-14 holds about <b>4.30×10²²</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 1.36×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Carbon-14</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">20 up quarks and 22 down quarks, weighing 146 MeV/c² together, out of 1.31×10⁴ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Carbon-14</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Carbon-14 atom (yours)</b><br><small>2.33×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.446824221250292%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {26, 30, "Cloud 3D", "Modern", "4f", false, "Iron-56 (Fe²⁵⁺)  ·  4fz³ orbital  ·  E −574.83 eV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 0.37 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 1,538 °C, boils at 2,861 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Iron-56</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>26 protons</b> and <b>30 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 26 protons always means iron. Protons plus neutrons make 56, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Iron has <b>26</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal iron atom. It is a iron nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Fe²⁵⁺</b>: a iron atom that has lost 25 of its 26 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>2.0 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Cloud 3D|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the same modern <b>quantum</b> picture as the Cloud view: a <b>probability cloud</b> that is denser where the electron is more likely to be found. Each dot is one place it could be found, and the cloud is a map of chances, not a path.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Here you can <b>drag to turn it</b> and see it from every side. The name, like <b>4f</b>, has two parts: the number is the energy level (n), and the letter is the shape (<b>s</b> sphere, <b>p</b> dumbbell, <b>d</b> lobes and rings, <b>f</b> even more lobes; the small letter after it says which way it points). The two colours are the + and − signs of the electron’s wave.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Iron-56 (Fe²⁵⁺) · 4fz³ orbital · E −574.83 eV · avg r: sampled …, theory 0.37 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 1,538 °C, boils at 2,861 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Iron-56 (Fe²⁵⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>4f orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For iron-56 the nucleus is about <b>9.2 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>443 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.6 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.1 times a bus. The whole atom would be 3.1 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3.3 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.3 times a bus. The whole atom would be 6.6 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>15 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 30 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>49 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 98 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>111 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.1 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 222 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg</b>. That is 55.9 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:46.38259242832904%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:53.59214682228235%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">26 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 46.4 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">30 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 53.6 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">26 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0253 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Iron-56 (0.0253 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Iron-56</b> on its own (26 protons, 30 neutrons, 26 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>56.5 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>55.9 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.528 u</b> (0.936 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (492 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">8.79 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.8×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.66666666666669%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Iron-56: 8.79 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Iron-56</b> atom weighs 9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.69×10²⁰ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.08×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.54×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Iron-56 holds about <b>1.08×10²²</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 3.41×10¹⁴ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Iron-56</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">82 up quarks and 86 down quarks, weighing 579 MeV/c² together, out of 5.26×10⁴ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Iron-56</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Iron-56 atom (yours)</b><br><small>9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:10.415350091251169%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {26, 30, "Orbit", "Bohr 1913", "1s", false, "Bohr model, 1913  ·  Iron  ·  nucleus +26  ·  n=1  ·  r 2.0 pm  ·  E −9.2 keV<br>Bohr’s rules: the electron circles only on the allowed orbits (n = 1, 2, 3 …) and does not give off light while it stays on one.<br>A jump to another orbit takes or gives exactly one photon, with the energy difference. The nucleus is drawn as one ball, not to scale: in 1913 nobody knew about neutrons (1932), protons as parts (1919) or quarks.",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Iron-56</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>26 protons</b> and <b>30 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 26 protons always means iron. Protons plus neutrons make 56, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Iron has <b>26</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal iron atom. It is a iron nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Fe²⁵⁺</b>: a iron atom that has lost 25 of its 26 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>2.0 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You are looking at the <b>1913 picture</b>.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">The nucleus is just one small ball with a plus sign, and the electron (minus sign) sits on one of the fixed circles n = 1, 2, 3, 4. That is how Niels Bohr drew it. Neutrons were not found until 1932, so they are not there. Use ↓ Emit photon and ↑ Absorb photon to see a jump: the electron leaps from one circle to another and one photon is given out or taken in. The nucleus ball is not to scale.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Bohr model, 1913 · Iron · nucleus +26 · n=1 · r 2.0 pm · E −9.2 keV<br>Bohr’s rules: the electron circles only on the allowed orbits (n = 1, 2, 3 …) and does not give off light while it stays on one.<br>A jump to another orbit takes or gives exactly one photon, with the energy difference. The nucleus is drawn as one ball, not to scale: in 1913 nobody knew about neutrons (1932), protons as parts (1919) or quarks.</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Iron-56 (Fe²⁵⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Fe²⁵⁺ means iron with 25 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +25.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For iron-56 the nucleus is about <b>9.2 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>443 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.6 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.1 times a bus. The whole atom would be 3.1 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3.3 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.3 times a bus. The whole atom would be 6.6 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>15 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 30 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>49 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 98 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>111 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.1 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 222 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">443 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 443 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 8.7×10⁷</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Iron atom has 26 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.6 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.0 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Iron-56</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg</b>. That is 55.9 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:46.38259242832904%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:53.59214682228235%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">26 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 46.4 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">30 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 53.6 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">26 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0253 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Iron-56 (0.0253 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Iron-56</b> on its own (26 protons, 30 neutrons, 26 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>56.5 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>55.9 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.528 u</b> (0.936 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (492 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">8.79 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.8×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.66666666666669%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Iron-56: 8.79 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Iron-56</b> atom weighs 9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.69×10²⁰ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.08×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.54×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Iron-56 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Iron-56 holds about <b>1.08×10²²</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 3.41×10¹⁴ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Iron-56</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">82 up quarks and 86 down quarks, weighing 579 MeV/c² together, out of 5.26×10⁴ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Iron-56</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Iron-56 atom (yours)</b><br><small>9.29×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:10.415350091251169%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {82, 126, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Lead-208 (Pb⁸¹⁺)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.65 pm  ·  E −91.5 keV  ·  period 23 zs<br>melts at 328 °C, boils at 1,749 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable<br>1s electron speed: 60% of light speed  ·  real 1s energy −102 keV vs −91.5 keV in this lab",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Lead-208</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>82 protons</b> and <b>126 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 82 protons always means lead. Protons plus neutrons make 208, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Lead has <b>82</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal lead atom. It is a lead nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Pb⁸¹⁺</b>: a lead atom that has lost 81 of its 82 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>0.6 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Lead-208 (Pb⁸¹⁺) · n=1 · r 0.65 pm · E −91.5 keV · period 23 zs<br>melts at 328 °C, boils at 1,749 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable<br>1s electron speed: 60% of light speed · real 1s energy −102 keV vs −91.5 keV in this lab</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Lead-208 (Pb⁸¹⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Pb⁸¹⁺ means lead with 81 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +81.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For lead-208 the nucleus is about <b>14 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>91 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>32 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.6×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 64 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">91 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 91 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lead atom has 82 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>68 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 5.7×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 1.4 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">91 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 91 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lead atom has 82 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>3.0 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.3 times a bus. The whole atom would be 6.1 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">91 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 91 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lead atom has 82 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>10.0 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times a bus. The whole atom would be 20 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">91 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 91 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lead atom has 82 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>23 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.9 times a bus. The whole atom would be 45 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">91 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 91 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lead atom has 82 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Lead-208</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>3.45×10⁻²⁵ kg</b>. That is 208 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:39.38173748641572%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:60.59681454896975%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">82 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 39.4 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">126 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 60.6 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">82 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0214 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Lead-208 (0.0214 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Lead-208</b> on its own (82 protons, 126 neutrons, 82 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>210 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>208 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>1.76 u</b> (0.838 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (1.64e+3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.87 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.6×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.41111111111111%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Lead-208: 7.87 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Lead-208</b> atom weighs 3.45×10⁻²⁵ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.24×10¹⁹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lead-208 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.90×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lead-208 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.03×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lead-208 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Lead-208 holds about <b>2.90×10²¹</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 9.17×10¹³ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Lead-208</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">290 up quarks and 334 down quarks, weighing 2.19e+3 MeV/c² together, out of 1.95×10⁵ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Lead-208</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Lead-208 atom (yours)</b><br><small>3.45×10⁻²⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:11.333758280744858%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {92, 146, "Cloud", "Modern", "1s", false, "Uranium-238 (U⁹¹⁺)  ·  1s orbital  ·  E −115 keV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 0.86 pm<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 1,132 °C, boils at 3,927 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 4.47 billion years)<br>1s electron speed: 67% of light speed  ·  real 1s energy −132 keV vs −115 keV in this lab",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Uranium-238</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>92 protons</b> and <b>146 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 92 protons always means uranium. Protons plus neutrons make 238, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>radioactive (half-life 4.47 billion years)</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Uranium has <b>92</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal uranium atom. It is a uranium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>U⁹¹⁺</b>: a uranium atom that has lost 91 of its 92 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>0.6 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Cloud|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the modern <b>quantum</b> picture (Erwin Schrödinger, 1926). An electron does not have an exact position or path. Instead you get a <b>probability cloud</b>: it is denser where the electron is more likely to be found.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Each dot is one place the electron could be found. Measure where an electron is, again and again, and the spots slowly build up this shape. So the cloud is a <b>map of chances</b>, not a picture of a path.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The name, like <b>1s</b>, has two parts. The number is the energy level (n). The letter is the shape: <b>s</b> is a sphere, <b>p</b> is a dumbbell, and <b>d</b> has more lobes and rings, and <b>f</b> has even more. A small letter after it, like <b>z</b> in 2p<sub>z</sub>, says which way the shape points. The two colours are the two signs of the electron’s wave: where they meet, the wave is zero and the electron is never found (a node). This is the <b>side view</b>: drag to move around.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Uranium-238 (U⁹¹⁺) · 1s orbital · E −115 keV · avg r: sampled …, theory 0.86 pm<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 1,132 °C, boils at 3,927 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 4.47 billion years)<br>1s electron speed: 67% of light speed · real 1s energy −132 keV vs −115 keV in this lab</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Uranium-238 (U⁹¹⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>1s orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For uranium-238 the nucleus is about <b>15 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>77 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>27 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.3×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 54 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">77 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 77 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.6×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Uranium atom has 92 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.4 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>58 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 4.8×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 1.2 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">77 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 77 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.6×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Uranium atom has 92 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.4 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.6 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 5.2 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">77 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 77 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.6×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Uranium atom has 92 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.4 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>8.5 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times a bus. The whole atom would be 17 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">77 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 77 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.6×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Uranium atom has 92 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.4 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>19 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.6 times a bus. The whole atom would be 39 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">77 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 77 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 4.6×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Uranium atom has 92 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.4 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Uranium-238</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>3.95×10⁻²⁵ kg</b>. That is 238 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:38.614680785956246%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:61.36428900156558%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">92 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 38.6 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">146 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 61.4 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">92 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.021 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Uranium-238 (0.021 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Uranium-238</b> on its own (92 protons, 146 neutrons, 92 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>240 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>238 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>1.93 u</b> (0.806 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (1.80e+3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.57 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.5×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:84.11111111111111%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uranium-238: 7.57 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Uranium-238</b> atom weighs 3.95×10⁻²⁵ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6.32×10¹⁹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Uranium-238 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.53×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Uranium-238 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.77×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Uranium-238 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Uranium-238 holds about <b>2.53×10²¹</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 8.02×10¹³ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Uranium-238</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">330 up quarks and 384 down quarks, weighing 2.51e+3 MeV/c² together, out of 2.23×10⁵ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Uranium-238</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Uranium-238 atom (yours)</b><br><small>3.95×10⁻²⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:11.428210593814061%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {118, 176, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Oganesson-294 (Og¹¹⁷⁺)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.45 pm  ·  E −189 keV  ·  period 11 zs<br>melting/boiling point: never measured (too few atoms made)<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 690 µs)<br>1s electron speed: 86% of light speed  ·  real 1s energy −251 keV vs −189 keV in this lab",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Oganesson-294</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>118 protons</b> and <b>176 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 118 protons always means oganesson. Protons plus neutrons make 294, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>radioactive (half-life 690 µs)</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Oganesson has <b>118</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal oganesson atom. It is a oganesson nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Og¹¹⁷⁺</b>: a oganesson atom that has lost 117 of its 118 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>0.4 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Oganesson-294 (Og¹¹⁷⁺) · n=1 · r 0.45 pm · E −189 keV · period 11 zs<br>melting/boiling point: never measured (too few atoms made)<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: radioactive (half-life 690 µs)<br>1s electron speed: 86% of light speed · real 1s energy −251 keV vs −189 keV in this lab</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Oganesson-294 (Og¹¹⁷⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Og¹¹⁷⁺ means oganesson with 117 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +117.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For oganesson-294 the nucleus is about <b>16 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>56 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>20 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.6×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 39 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">56 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 56 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 1.8×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Oganesson atom has 118 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (8.0 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.4 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>42 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 3.5×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 84 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">56 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 56 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 1.8×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Oganesson atom has 118 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (8.0 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.4 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.9 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 3.8 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">56 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 56 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 1.8×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Oganesson atom has 118 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (8.0 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.4 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>6.2 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a bus. The whole atom would be 12 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">56 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 56 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 1.8×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Oganesson atom has 118 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (8.0 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.4 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>14 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 28 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">56 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 56 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 1.8×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Oganesson atom has 118 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (8.0 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.4 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Oganesson-294</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>4.89×10⁻²⁵ kg</b>. That is 294 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:40.09420545366808%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:59.883958559534385%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">118 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 40.1 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">176 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 59.9 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">118 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0218 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Oganesson-294 (0.0218 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Oganesson-294</b> on its own (118 protons, 176 neutrons, 118 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>296 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>294 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>2.23 u</b> (0.754 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (2.08e+3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.08 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.4×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.65555555555557%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Oganesson-294: 7.08 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Oganesson-294</b> atom weighs 4.89×10⁻²⁵ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">5.12×10¹⁹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Oganesson-294 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.05×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Oganesson-294 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.43×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Oganesson-294 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Oganesson-294 holds about <b>2.05×10²¹</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 6.49×10¹³ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Oganesson-294</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">412 up quarks and 470 down quarks, weighing 3.08e+3 MeV/c² together, out of 2.76×10⁵ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Oganesson-294</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Oganesson-294 atom (yours)</b><br><small>4.89×10⁻²⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:11.576348739758725%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {4, 0, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Beryllium-4 (Be³⁺)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.13 Å  ·  E −217.69 eV  ·  period 9 as<br>melts at 1,287 °C, boils at 2,469 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Beryllium-4</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>4 protons</b> and <b>no neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 4 protons always means beryllium. Protons plus neutrons make 4, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Beryllium has <b>4</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal beryllium atom. It is a beryllium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Be³⁺</b>: a beryllium atom that has lost 3 of its 4 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>13 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Beryllium-4 (Be³⁺) · n=1 · r 0.13 Å · E −217.69 eV · period 9 as<br>melts at 1,287 °C, boils at 2,469 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Beryllium-4 (Be³⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Be³⁺ means beryllium with 3 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +3.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For beryllium-4 the nucleus is about <b>3.8 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>6,945 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>24 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.0 times a bus. The whole atom would be 49 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6,945 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6,945 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 3.3×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Beryllium atom has 4 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (13 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>52 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 104 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6,945 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6,945 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 3.3×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Beryllium atom has 4 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (13 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>233 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 465 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6,945 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6,945 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 3.3×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Beryllium atom has 4 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (13 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>764 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.8 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 1.5 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6,945 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6,945 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 3.3×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Beryllium atom has 4 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (13 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.7 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.7 times a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 3.5 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">6,945 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 6,945 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 3.3×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.95 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Beryllium atom has 4 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (13 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Beryllium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>6.83×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 4.11 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:99.94556794236551%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">4 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 99.9 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">0 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">This atom has none.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">4 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0544 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Beryllium-4 (0.0544 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Beryllium-4</b> on its own (4 protons, 0 neutrons, 4 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>4.03 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>4.11 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0 u</b> (0 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (0 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">-19.11 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">0× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:1%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Beryllium-4: -19.11 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron. <b>Nobody has measured this isotope’s mass, so the number is an estimate from a formula.</b></p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Beryllium-4</b> atom weighs 6.83×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.66×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Beryllium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.46×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Beryllium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.02×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Beryllium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Beryllium-4 holds about <b>1.46×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 4.64×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Beryllium-4</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.0 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">8 up quarks and 4 down quarks, weighing 36 MeV/c² together, out of 3.75e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Beryllium-4</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Beryllium-4 atom (yours)</b><br><small>6.83×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.590089835521876%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {3, 1, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Lithium-4 (Li²⁺)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.18 Å  ·  E −122.45 eV  ·  period 17 as<br>melts at 181 °C, boils at 1,342 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: unbound: falls apart almost instantly (~10⁻²² s)",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Lithium-4</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>3 protons</b> and <b>1 neutron</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 3 protons always means lithium. Protons plus neutrons make 4, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>unbound: falls apart almost instantly (~10⁻²² s)</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Lithium has <b>3</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal lithium atom. It is a lithium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Li²⁺</b>: a lithium atom that has lost 2 of its 3 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>18 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Lithium-4 (Li²⁺) · n=1 · r 0.18 Å · E −122.45 eV · period 17 as<br>melts at 181 °C, boils at 1,342 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: unbound: falls apart almost instantly (~10⁻²² s)</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Lithium-4 (Li²⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Li²⁺ means lithium with 2 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +2.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For lithium-4 the nucleus is about <b>3.8 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>9,260 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>32 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 2.7 times a bus. The whole atom would be 65 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9,260 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 9,260 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.9×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.96 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lithium atom has 3 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (18 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>69 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 139 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9,260 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 9,260 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.9×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.96 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lithium atom has 3 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (18 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>310 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.9 times the Eiffel Tower. The whole atom would be 620 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9,260 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 9,260 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.9×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.96 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lithium atom has 3 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (18 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.0 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about the same as a 1 km walk. The whole atom would be 2.0 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9,260 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 9,260 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.9×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.96 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lithium atom has 3 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (18 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.3 km</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a 5 km run. The whole atom would be 4.6 km wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9,260 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 9,260 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 7.9×10¹¹</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.96 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Lithium atom has 3 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (1.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (18 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Lithium-4</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>6.69×10⁻²⁷ kg</b>. That is 4.03 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:74.94356236851131%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:25.015622091688787%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 74.9 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 25 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0408 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Lithium-4 (0.0408 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Lithium-4</b> on its own (3 protons, 1 neutron, 3 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>4.03 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>4.03 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>4.96×10⁻³ u</b> (0.123 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (4.62 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.15 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.3×10⁵× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:12.822222222222221%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Lithium-4: 1.15 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Lithium-4</b> atom weighs 6.69×10⁻²⁷ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.74×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lithium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.50×10²³ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lithium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.05×10²⁸ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Lithium-4 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Lithium-4 holds about <b>1.50×10²³</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 4.74×10¹⁵ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Lithium-4</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.0 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">7 up quarks and 5 down quarks, weighing 38.5 MeV/c² together, out of 3.75e+3 MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Lithium-4</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Lithium-4 atom (yours)</b><br><small>6.69×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:8.575282815783183%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {50, 70, "Slice", "Modern", "1s", false, "Tin-120 (Sn⁴⁹⁺)  ·  1s orbital  ·  E −34.0 keV  ·  avg r: sampled …, theory 1.6 pm<br>cut plane: y = 0.00 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 232 °C, boils at 2,602 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: stable<br>1s electron speed: 36% of light speed  ·  real 1s energy −35.2 keV vs −34.0 keV in this lab",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Tin-120</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>50 protons</b> and <b>70 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 50 protons always means tin. Protons plus neutrons make 120, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Tin has <b>50</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal tin atom. It is a tin nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Sn⁴⁹⁺</b>: a tin atom that has lost 49 of its 50 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>1.1 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Slice|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the same quantum cloud, but <b>cut in half</b>, like slicing a fruit and looking at the flat cut. The brightness shows how likely the electron is to be found at each spot on that cut.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Blue and orange are the two <b>signs</b> (+ and −) of the electron’s wave, called ψ. Where they meet, the wave passes through zero. That is called a <b>node</b>, and the electron is never found exactly there.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Drag up or down on the picture, or use the cut controls, to move the cut through the cloud. The name, like <b>1s</b>, has the energy level (n) as a number and the shape as a letter.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Tin-120 (Sn⁴⁹⁺) · 1s orbital · E −34.0 keV · avg r: sampled …, theory 1.6 pm<br>cut plane: y = 0.00 Å<br>two colours = the + and − halves of the wave ψ (zero where they meet)<br>melts at 232 °C, boils at 2,602 °C · solid at room temperature<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: stable<br>1s electron speed: 36% of light speed · real 1s energy −35.2 keV vs −34.0 keV in this lab</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Tin-120 (Sn⁴⁹⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron.</li><li><b>1s orbital</b>: which cloud shape is shown.</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>: the number is how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>avg r</b>: the average distance from the nucleus. <b>sampled</b> is measured from the dots on screen, and <b>theory</b> is what the equations say it should be. They differ a little because the dots are random.</li><li><b>cut plane</b>: where the knife went through the cloud.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For tin-120 the nucleus is about <b>12 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>179 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>63 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 5.2×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 1.3 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">179 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 179 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 5.7×10⁶</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Tin atom has 50 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (5.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (1.1 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>1.3 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.1 times a bus. The whole atom would be 2.7 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">179 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 179 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 5.7×10⁶</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Tin atom has 50 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (5.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (1.1 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>6.0 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.5 times a bus. The whole atom would be 12 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">179 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 179 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 5.7×10⁶</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Tin atom has 50 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (5.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (1.1 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>20 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.6 times a bus. The whole atom would be 39 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">179 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 179 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 5.7×10⁶</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Tin atom has 50 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (5.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (1.1 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>45 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.4 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 89 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">179 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 179 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 5.7×10⁶</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Tin atom has 50 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (5.9 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (1.1 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Tin-120</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>1.99×10⁻²⁵ kg</b>. That is 120 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:41.623752425584975%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:58.35357857029972%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">50 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 41.6 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">70 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 58.4 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">50 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0227 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Tin-120 (0.0227 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Tin-120</b> on its own (50 protons, 70 neutrons, 50 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>121 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>120 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>1.1 u</b> (0.905 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (1.02e+3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">8.50 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.7×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:94.48888888888888%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Tin-120: 8.50 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Tin-120</b> atom weighs 1.99×10⁻²⁵ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.26×10²⁰ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Tin-120 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">5.02×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Tin-120 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.52×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Tin-120 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Tin-120 holds about <b>5.02×10²¹</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 1.59×10¹⁴ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Tin-120</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">170 up quarks and 190 down quarks, weighing 1.25e+3 MeV/c² together, out of 1.13×10⁵ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Tin-120</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Tin-120 atom (yours)</b><br><small>1.99×10⁻²⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:10.94859337703033%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {110, 150, "Orbit", "Modern", "1s", false, "Darmstadtium-260 (Ds¹⁰⁹⁺)  ·  n=1  ·  r 0.48 pm  ·  E −165 keV  ·  period 13 zs<br>melting/boiling point: never measured (too few atoms made)<br>layer: Atom  ·  this nucleus in real life: never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet<br>1s electron speed: 80% of light speed  ·  real 1s energy −206 keV vs −165 keV in this lab",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Darmstadtium-260</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>110 protons</b> and <b>150 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 110 protons always means darmstadtium. Protons plus neutrons make 260, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Darmstadtium has <b>110</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal darmstadtium atom. It is a darmstadtium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Ds¹⁰⁹⁺</b>: a darmstadtium atom that has lost 109 of its 110 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>0.5 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Darmstadtium-260 (Ds¹⁰⁹⁺) · n=1 · r 0.48 pm · E −165 keV · period 13 zs<br>melting/boiling point: never measured (too few atoms made)<br>layer: Atom · this nucleus in real life: never observed: it either falls apart instantly or nobody has made it yet<br>1s electron speed: 80% of light speed · real 1s energy −206 keV vs −165 keV in this lab</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Darmstadtium-260 (Ds¹⁰⁹⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Ds¹⁰⁹⁺ means darmstadtium with 109 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +109.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For darmstadtium-260 the nucleus is about <b>15 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>63 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>22 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.8×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 44 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">63 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 63 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Darmstadtium atom has 110 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.7 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.5 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>47 cm</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 3.9×10⁻² times a bus. The whole atom would be 94 cm wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">63 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 63 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Darmstadtium atom has 110 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.7 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.5 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.1 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 4.2 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">63 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 63 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Darmstadtium atom has 110 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.7 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.5 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>6.9 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.6 times a bus. The whole atom would be 14 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">63 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 63 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Darmstadtium atom has 110 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.7 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.5 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>16 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.3 times a bus. The whole atom would be 31 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">63 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 63 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.5×10⁵</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.98 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Darmstadtium atom has 110 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (7.7 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (0.5 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Darmstadtium-260</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>4.32×10⁻²⁵ kg</b>. That is 260 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:42.26434351388618%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:57.71263860518728%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">110 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 42.3 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">150 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 57.7 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">110 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.023 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Darmstadtium-260 (0.023 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Darmstadtium-260</b> on its own (110 protons, 150 neutrons, 110 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>262 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>260 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>2.03 u</b> (0.774 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (1.89e+3 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">7.27 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.5×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:80.7296836302282%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Darmstadtium-260: 7.27 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron. <b>Nobody has measured this isotope’s mass, so the number is an estimate from a formula.</b></p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Darmstadtium-260</b> atom weighs 4.32×10⁻²⁵ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">5.79×10¹⁹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Darmstadtium-260 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">2.32×10²¹ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Darmstadtium-260 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.62×10²⁶ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Darmstadtium-260 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Darmstadtium-260 holds about <b>2.32×10²¹</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 7.34×10¹³ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Darmstadtium-260</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">370 up quarks and 410 down quarks, weighing 2.71e+3 MeV/c² together, out of 2.44×10⁵ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Darmstadtium-260</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Darmstadtium-260 atom (yours)</b><br><small>4.32×10⁻²⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:11.490247487622765%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
  {20, 20, "Orbit", "Bohr 1913", "1s", false, "Bohr model, 1913  ·  Calcium  ·  nucleus +20  ·  n=1  ·  r 2.6 pm  ·  E −5.4 keV<br>Bohr’s rules: the electron circles only on the allowed orbits (n = 1, 2, 3 …) and does not give off light while it stays on one.<br>A jump to another orbit takes or gives exactly one photon, with the energy difference. The nucleus is drawn as one ball, not to scale: in 1913 nobody knew about neutrons (1932), protons as parts (1919) or quarks.",
   {"big|🔎 The big picture|<p class=\"lrnP\">Everything around you is made of <b>atoms</b>. This lab shows you one, enlarged hundreds of millions of times so you can see how it is built.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">An atom has two main parts: a tiny, very heavy <b>nucleus</b> in the middle, and light <b>electrons</b> around it. The nucleus holds almost all of the atom’s weight. The electrons fill almost all of its size, and in between is mostly empty space.</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You have <b>Calcium-40</b> open.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Its nucleus has <b>20 protons</b> and <b>20 neutrons</b>. The number of protons decides which element it is: 20 protons always means calcium. Protons plus neutrons make 40, which is the number in the name.</div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Real life: this nucleus is <b>stable</b>.</p>",
    "nuc|🔴 The nucleus: protons and neutrons|<p class=\"lrnP\">The nucleus is made of two kinds of particles, together called <b>nucleons</b>:</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">• <b>Protons</b> have a positive electric charge (+1).<br>• <b>Neutrons</b> have no electric charge (0) and are a tiny bit heavier than protons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Different numbers of neutrons make different versions of the same element, called <b>isotopes</b>. That is why carbon can be carbon-12, carbon-13 or carbon-14.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why doesn’t it fly apart?</b> Protons all have a positive charge, and the same charges push each other away. What holds the nucleus together is the <b>strong nuclear force</b>. At the tiny distances inside a nucleus it is far stronger than the electric push, but it only reaches across about the width of a proton or two.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">In the Nucleus zoom, the reddish glowing blobs are protons and the grey-white ones are neutrons. The colours and glow are just there to help you tell them apart.</p>",
    "quark|🧩 Inside a proton or neutron: quarks|<p class=\"lrnP\">Protons and neutrons are not the smallest pieces. Each one is made of three <b>quarks</b>, held together by the strong force. Particles called <b>gluons</b> carry that force.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Only two kinds of quark are needed to build protons and neutrons. An <b>up</b> quark has a charge of +⅔ and a <b>down</b> quark has a charge of −⅓:</p> <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Proton = up + up + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ + ⅔ − ⅓ = <b>+1</b></div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"margin-top:8px\">Neutron = up + down + down</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">+⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓ = <b>0</b></div></div> <p class=\"lrnP\">Surprise: the three quarks weigh only about <b>1%</b> of a proton. The other 99% or so is the energy of the strong force holding them together.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The small coloured dots in the deepest zoom are the quarks. Their red, green and blue are not real colours. “Colour charge” is just the name physicists gave to the strong force’s kind of charge. Real nucleons also contain a fizz of gluons and short-lived quark pairs, which you can switch on under Nucleon detail.</p>",
    "elec|⚡ The electron|<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>electron</b> has a negative charge (−1), exactly as big as a proton’s charge but the opposite sign. That is why an atom with as many electrons as protons has no charge overall. An electron is also very light: a proton weighs about 1,836 times more.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Opposite charges attract, so the nucleus pulls on the electron and keeps it around. But the electron can only have certain amounts of energy, called <b>energy levels</b> (n = 1, 2, 3 …). Level n = 1 is the lowest, so the electron cannot sink any further.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">Give an electron the right amount of energy, for example from a particle of light (a <b>photon</b>), and it jumps up a level. When it drops back down it gives that energy out as light. You can try this in the Photons card.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">As far as anyone has measured, an electron has no size at all. It is a point.</p>",
    "one|☝ Why only one electron?|<p class=\"lrnP\">A real, neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Calcium has <b>20</b>. But this lab draws just <b>one</b>.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">That means what you see is not a normal calcium atom. It is a calcium nucleus with a single electron, called <b>Ca¹⁹⁺</b>: a calcium atom that has lost 19 of its 20 electrons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Why?</b> With one electron the physics can be worked out exactly, on paper. With two or more, the electrons also push on each other, and the equations have no exact solution. So the lab shows the case that can be drawn correctly.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\">The more protons, the stronger the pull, so the electron sits <b>closer</b> and is held <b>tighter</b>. For this nucleus the lowest orbit is about <b>2.6 pm</b> from the nucleus (a pm is a trillionth of a metre).</p>",
    "pic|🖼 The picture on screen: Orbit|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">You are looking at the <b>1913 picture</b>.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">The nucleus is just one small ball with a plus sign, and the electron (minus sign) sits on one of the fixed circles n = 1, 2, 3, 4. That is how Niels Bohr drew it. Neutrons were not found until 1932, so they are not there. Use ↓ Emit photon and ↑ Absorb photon to see a jump: the electron leaps from one circle to another and one photon is given out or taken in. The nucleus ball is not to scale.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">This is the <b>Bohr model</b>, made by Niels Bohr in 1913. The electron goes round the nucleus on a circle, like a planet round a star. The dotted trail behind it shows where it has just been.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>It is a simplification.</b> The Bohr model gets the <b>energies</b> of a one-electron atom right, and that is why it correctly predicts the light hydrogen gives out. But real electrons do not move along neat circles. The Cloud views show the modern picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">A real electron goes round far too fast to see, so the lab slows time down by a huge factor. The exact number is written under the picture.</p> <p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Drag the picture to turn the atom and look at the orbit from another angle.</p>",
    "num|🔢 Reading the numbers at the top|<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnSm\">Right now the lab says:</div><div class=\"lrnBig\" style=\"font-size:14px\">Bohr model, 1913 · Calcium · nucleus +20 · n=1 · r 2.6 pm · E −5.4 keV<br>Bohr’s rules: the electron circles only on the allowed orbits (n = 1, 2, 3 …) and does not give off light while it stays on one.<br>A jump to another orbit takes or gives exactly one photon, with the energy difference. The nucleus is drawn as one ball, not to scale: in 1913 nobody knew about neutrons (1932), protons as parts (1919) or quarks.</div></div><ul class=\"lrnUl\"><li><b>Calcium-40 (Ca¹⁹⁺)</b>: the atom, with its one electron. Ca¹⁹⁺ means calcium with 19 electrons missing, so it has a charge of +19.</li><li><b>n=…</b>: the energy level. 1 is the lowest.</li><li><b>r</b>: how far the electron is from the nucleus. Å is an ångström (0.1 nanometre) and pm is a picometre (0.01 Å).</li><li><b>E</b>: the electron’s energy, in <b>eV</b> (electronvolts, a handy tiny unit). It is negative because the electron is <i>held</i>. The number tells you how much energy it takes to pull it away completely.</li><li><b>period</b>: the time for one trip round. <b>as</b> means attoseconds: a billionth of a billionth of a second.</li></ul>",
    "scale|📏 What is to scale, and what is not|<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the lab draws the electron’s orbit and the nucleus at the <i>same</i> scale. That is why the nucleus is only a dot in the middle. For calcium-40 the nucleus is about <b>8.2 fm</b> across (1 fm is a millionth of a billionth of a metre), and the lowest orbit is about <b>645 times</b> bigger than the nucleus’s radius. Zoom in to find it: use the Nucleus and Nucleon buttons.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours, the glow and the sparkle. Real protons and neutrons have no colour. Light waves are far too long to show anything that small. So the honest picture would be <span class=\"monoKey\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" id=\"monoKey\">monochrome</span>: just shades of grey.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the electron drawn as a little ball (as far as anyone can tell, it is really a point), the neat circle in the Orbit view, and only one electron.</p> <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Slowed down:</b> everything happens far too fast to watch, so the lab slows time.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">Each step down the list is far smaller than the one above. The bars are on a <b>log scale</b>: every tick to the left is 10 times smaller.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Human hair, width<br><small>7.0×10⁻⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:97.58570095247794%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Red blood cell<br><small>8.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:91.17748290470712%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁶ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:87.08183670519715%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Virus<br><small>1.0×10⁻⁷ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:78.2312925170068%;background:#8a6ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">DNA, width of the helix<br><small>2.0×10⁻⁹ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:66.67367343989103%;background:#4a8ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Hydrogen atom<br><small>1.1×10⁻¹⁰ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:57.99527799499844%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Uranium-238 nucleus<br><small>1.5×10⁻¹⁴ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:31.810144619426406%;background:#d27a2a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.7×10⁻¹⁵ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:25.37720354679098%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Quark: smaller than<br><small>1.0×10⁻¹⁸ m</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:3.4013605442176873%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From a hair to a quark is a factor of about <b>100 trillion</b> (10¹⁴).</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a pea</b> (7.0 mm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>2.3 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.2 times a bus. The whole atom would be 4.5 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">645 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 645 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10⁸</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Calcium atom has 20 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a marble</b> (1.5 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>4.8 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.4 times a bus. The whole atom would be 9.7 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">645 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 645 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10⁸</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Calcium atom has 20 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a tennis ball</b> (6.7 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>22 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.8 times a bus. The whole atom would be 43 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">645 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 645 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10⁸</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Calcium atom has 20 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a football</b> (22 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>71 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 0.7 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 142 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">645 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 645 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10⁸</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Calcium atom has 20 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Atoms are almost entirely empty space. Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. Pick an object and imagine the nucleus were that size.</p>[[chips]]<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">If the nucleus were <b>a beach ball</b> (50 cm across), the electron's orbit would be <b>161 m</b> away.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">That is about 1.5 times a football pitch. The whole atom would be 322 m wide.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Atom : nucleus width</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">645 : 1</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The electron in the 1s orbit is 645 nucleus-radii away.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Filled with matter</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1 part in 2.7×10⁸</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Volume of the nucleus compared to the volume of the orbit. The rest is empty space.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Mass in the nucleus</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">99.97 %</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">A neutral Calcium atom has 20 electrons, and together they weigh almost nothing next to the nucleus.</div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Uses the real size of the nucleus (4.1 femtometres) and the lab's 1s orbit (2.6 picometres). In bigger atoms the outer electrons sit further out, so the real empty space is even larger. Change the element in the lab and reopen this panel to compare.</p>"},
   {"<p class=\"lrnP\">What does an atom weigh, and how much of that is each part? Here is <b>Calcium-40</b>, the one you have open in the lab. One atom weighs <b>6.64×10⁻²⁶ kg</b>. That is 40 atomic mass units (u), where 1 u is about the weight of one proton.</p><div class=\"lrnStack\"><i style=\"background:#d24a4a;width:49.95197018889652%\"></i><i style=\"background:#8a8a99;width:50.02082511782638%\"></i><i style=\"background:#4aa8c0;width:0.4%\"></i></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#d24a4a\"></span>Protons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">20 × 1.6726×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#8a8a99\"></span>Neutrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">20 × 1.6749×10⁻²⁷ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 50 % of the weight.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\"><span class=\"lrnDot\" style=\"background:#4aa8c0\"></span>Electrons</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">20 × 9.1094×10⁻³¹ kg</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Together 0.0272 % of the weight.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">A proton is <b>1,836 times</b> heavier than an electron.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">If a proton were a 1 kg bag of flour, an electron would weigh only <b>0.54 g</b>, about half a paperclip. That is why the electrons in Calcium-40 (0.0272 % of its weight) hardly count, and why nearly all of an atom’s weight sits in the tiny nucleus.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">The neutron is a tiny bit heavier than the proton: <b>0.14 %</b> more (1.293 MeV/c²). That small gap matters: it is why a neutron alone falls apart into a proton, an electron and a neutrino after about 15 minutes.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Here is a strange fact. Weigh every part of <b>Calcium-40</b> on its own (20 protons, 20 neutrons, 20 electrons), add them up, and you get <b>more</b> than the real atom weighs.</p><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">The parts add up to <b>40.3 u</b>, but the atom weighs <b>40 u</b>.<br>Missing: <b>0.367 u</b> (0.91 % of the weight).</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Where did it go? Einstein’s <b>E = mc²</b>: when the nucleons snapped together, that mass left as energy (342 MeV), and holding it back out again costs the same energy. It is called the <b>binding energy</b>. A nucleus weighs less than its parts because it is <i>held together</i>.</div></div><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Binding energy per nucleon</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">8.55 MeV</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">The energy needed to pull out one proton or neutron on average.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Compared with chemistry</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.7×10⁶× a chemical bond</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Per particle. A typical chemical bond is about 5 eV, so the forces inside a nucleus are millions of times stronger.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnTr\" style=\"height:16px\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:95.01111111111112%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Calcium-40: 8.55 MeV per nucleon on a bar that ends at 9. The most tightly bound nuclei are around iron and nickel (about 8.8). Lighter nuclei can release energy by <b>fusing</b> (that is how the Sun shines) and heavier ones by <b>splitting</b> (fission). That is also why a star gets no more energy from fusing atoms past iron.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">One <b>Calcium-40</b> atom weighs 6.64×10⁻²⁶ kg. So how many of them make up everyday things?</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A grain of rice (25 mg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">3.77×10²⁰ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Calcium-40 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A paperclip (1 g)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.51×10²² atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Calcium-40 atoms weigh as much.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">A person (70 kg)</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">1.05×10²⁷ atoms</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">That many Calcium-40 atoms weigh as much.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">One gram of Calcium-40 holds about <b>1.51×10²²</b> atoms.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Count one atom every second and you would need about 4.77×10¹⁴ years. The Universe is 13.8 billion years old (1.4×10¹⁰ years).</div></div>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">A proton is made of three quarks (up, up, down). You might think its weight is the weight of those three quarks. It is not.</p><div class=\"lrnFacts\"><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Proton weight</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">938.3 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Measured.</div></div><div class=\"lrnF\"><div class=\"lrnFa\">Its 3 quarks added up</div><div class=\"lrnFb\">9.0 MeV/c²</div><div class=\"lrnFc\">Up ≈ 2.2 and down ≈ 4.7 MeV/c², about 1.0 % of the proton.</div></div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">About <b>99 %</b> of a proton’s weight is not quarks at all.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">It is the <b>energy</b> of the gluons and of the quarks rushing around inside, and by E = mc² energy has weight. The Higgs field explains the small quark masses (and the whole electron), but most of the weight of everything you can touch comes from the strong force.</div></div><div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">In <b>Calcium-40</b> the quarks add up to only <b>1.1 %</b> of the nucleons’ weight.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">60 up quarks and 60 down quarks, weighing 410 MeV/c² together, out of 3.76×10⁴ MeV/c².</div></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Quark masses are approximate (2.16 and 4.67 MeV/c², from the Particle Data Group) and depend on how you define them.</p>",
    "<p class=\"lrnP\">Weights from an electron to the Sun, on a <b>log scale</b>: each tick to the left is 10 times lighter. <b>Calcium-40</b> is highlighted.</p><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Electron<br><small>9.11×10⁻³¹ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:2.350223825365912%;background:#4aa8c0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Proton<br><small>1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:7.606115594187571%;background:#d24a4a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Water molecule<br><small>2.99×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:9.622658918396828%;background:#5a8ad0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\"><b>Calcium-40 atom (yours)</b><br><small>6.64×10⁻²⁶ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:10.180197869893359%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Bacterium (about 1 picogram)<br><small>1.00×10⁻¹⁵ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:26.570048309178745%;background:#5aa05a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Grain of salt (about 0.06 mg)<br><small>6.00×10⁻⁸ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:39.09525161092374%;background:#c9c9c9\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Paperclip (1 g)<br><small>1.00×10⁻³ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:45.893719806763286%;background:#a0a0b0\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Apple (about 200 g)<br><small>0.2 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:49.599082118621546%;background:#d2503a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">A person (70 kg)<br><small>70 kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:53.69581004833214%;background:#c9a06a\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">Earth<br><small>5.97×10²⁴ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:90.62153676510366%;background:#3a7ad2\"></div></div></div><div class=\"lrnRow\"><div class=\"lrnRn\">The Sun<br><small>1.99×10³⁰ kg</small></div><div class=\"lrnTr\"><div class=\"lrnBar\" style=\"width:99.51506131466941%;background:#e0b020\"></div></div></div><p class=\"lrnP\">From an electron to the Sun is a factor of about <b>2×10⁶⁰</b>.</p>"}},
};
