  {26, 30, false, 1811.4000000000003, {}, "● Iron-56: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:215\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>26</small><b>Fe</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Iron</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>transition metal</span><span>period 4 · group 8</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> Steel, and the haemoglobin in your blood that carries oxygen. Earth’s core is mostly iron and nickel.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>1,811 K</b> · 1,538 °C · 2,800 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>3,134 K</b> · 2,861 °C · 5,182 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 🧊 solid · density 7.87 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:54.93936051077891%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:17.33327146933742%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Iron with only one electron left (Fe²⁵⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal iron atom, with all 26 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Iron the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>1,811 K</b> · 1,538 °C