  {31, 39, false, 310, {"Bi-212"}, "☢ Gallium-70: half-life 21.2 minutes · decays by beta minus (β⁻) 100 %, beta plus or electron capture (β⁺/EC) 0.41 %", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:185\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>31</small><b>Ga</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Gallium</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>post-transition metal</span><span>period 4 · group 13</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> Melts in your hand (about 30 °C). Used in LEDs and phone chips.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>303 K</b> · 30 °C · 86 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>2,477 K</b> · 2,204 °C · 3,999 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 🧊 solid · density 5.91 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:22.47220505424423%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:41.779902563586745%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Gallium melts just above room temperature: it would melt in your hand or on a hot day.</div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Gallium with only one electron left (Ga³⁰⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal gallium atom, with all 31 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Gallium the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>310 K</b> · 37 °C · 98 °F  →  Gallium is <b>💧 liquid</b>", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>335 m/s</b> (≈ 1,205 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNone|Bi-212: no decays yet. When it decays, each step of the chain is listed here with how long it lived."}},
  {33, 42, false, 900.0000000000001, {"Fe-56"}, "● Arsenic-75: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:150\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>33</small><b>As</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Arsenic</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>metalloid</span><span>period 4 · group 15</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> A famous poison in old detective stories. Also used in some chips (gallium arsenide).</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> none at normal pressure. It <b>sublimes</b> (solid → gas) at <b>887 K</b> · 614 °C · 1,137 °F.</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 🧊 solid · density 5.75 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:38.44910055298216%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Under pressure arsenic melts at about 1,090 K.</div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Arsenic with only one electron left (As³²⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal arsenic atom, with all 33 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Arsenic the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>900 K</b> · 627 °C · 1,160 °F  →  Arsenic is <b>☁️ gas</b> (it sublimed)", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>547 m/s</b> (≈ 1,970 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNone|Fe-56 does not decay (as far as anyone has measured). Build an unstable nucleus to start a decay history."}},
  {35, 44, false, 270, {"Bi-212", "β⁻|3600.5|Po-212", "α|3e-7|Pb-208"}, "● Bromine-79: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:70\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>35</small><b>Br</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Bromine</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>halogen</span><span>period 4 · group 17</span><span>💧 liquid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> One of only two elements that are liquid at room temperature. Its name means “stench”.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>266 K</b> · −7 °C · 19 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>332 K</b> · 59 °C · 138 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 💧 liquid · density 3.1 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:21.05548226313834%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:2.467556089364792%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Bromine is one of only two elements that are liquid at room temperature.</div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Bromine with only one electron left (Br³⁴⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal bromine atom, with all 35 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Bromine the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>270 K</b> · −3 °C · 26 °F  →  Bromine is <b>💧 liquid</b>", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>292 m/s</b> (≈ 1,051 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNuc|Bi-212", "decStep|↓ β⁻, after 1 hour", "decNuc|Po-212", "decStep|↓ α, after 3.0×10⁻⁷ s", "decNuc|Pb-208", "decNone|2 decays in 1 hour of real time"}},
  {43, 55, false, 2500, {"U-238", "split in two (fission)|120000000000000000|"}, "☢ Technetium-98: half-life 4.21 million years · decays by beta minus (β⁻)", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:215\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>43</small><b>Tc</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Technetium</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>transition metal</span><span>period 5 · group 7</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span><span>☢ radioactive: no stable isotope</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> The first element made by humans. One of its forms, technetium-99m, is used in millions of medical scans every year.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>2,430 K</b> · 2,157 °C · 3,914 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>4,538 K</b> · 4,265 °C · 7,709 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 🧊 solid · density 11 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:63.63961030678927%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:23.32781654564401%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Technetium with only one electron left (Tc⁴²⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal technetium atom, with all 43 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightl