es are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>302 K</b> · 28 °C · 83 °F  →  Cesium is <b>🧊 solid</b>", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>238 m/s</b> (≈ 856 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNuc|Og-294", "decStep|↓ α, after 700 µs", "decNuc|something this lab cannot build", "decNone|1 decay in 700 µs of real time"}},
  {80, 122, false, 250, {}, "● Mercury-202: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:215\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>80</small><b>Hg</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Mercury</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>transition metal</span><span>period 6 · group 12</span><span>💧 liquid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> The liquid metal in old thermometers. It is poisonous.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>234 K</b> · −39 °C · −38 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>630 K</b> · 357 °C · 674 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 💧 liquid · density 13.5 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:19.748417658131498%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:12.6552858339078%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Mercury is one of only two elements that are liquid at room temperature.</div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Mercury with only one electron left (Hg⁷⁹⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal mercury atom, with all 80 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Mercury the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these a