  {104, 163, false, 2000, {"U-238", "split in two (fission)|120000000000000000|"}, "☢ Rutherfordium-267: half-life 48 minutes · decays by spontaneous fission", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:215\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>104</small><b>Rf</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Rutherfordium</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>transition metal</span><span>period 7 · group 4</span><span>state at room temperature unknown</span><span>☢ radioactive: no stable isotope</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> Made a few atoms at a time in particle accelerators. The atoms decay again quickly, often within seconds.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting and boiling point:</b> unknown. Only a few atoms of Rutherfordium have ever been made, so nobody has measured them (heavy elements like this are usually predicted to be solid).</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Rutherfordium with only one electron left (Rf¹⁰³⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal rutherfordium atom, with all 104 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Rutherfordium the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>2,000 K</b> · 1,727 °C · 3,140 °F  →  Rutherfordium is <b>unknown</b> (never measured)", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>432 m/s</b> (≈ 1,556 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNuc|U-238", "decStep|↓ split in two (fission), after 3.8 billion years", "decNuc|✂ two smaller nuclei", "decNone|1 decay in 3.8 billion years of real time"}},
  {118, 176, false, 20.000000000000004, {"Og-294", "α|0.0007|"}, "☢ Oganesson-294: half-life 690 µs · decays by alpha (α)", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:270\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>118</small><b>Og</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Oganesson</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>noble gas</span><span>period 7 · group 18</span><span>state at room temperature unknown</span><span>☢ radioactive: no stable isotope</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> The heaviest element known. Only a handful of atoms have ever been made.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting and boiling point:</b> unknown. Only a few atoms of Oganesson have ever been made, so nobody has measured them (heavy elements like this are usually predicted to be solid).</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Oganesson with only one electron left (Og¹¹⁷⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal oganesson atom, with all 118 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Oganesson the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>20.0 K</b> · −253 °C · −424 °F  →  Oganesson is <b>unknown</b> (never measured)", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>41 m/s</b> (≈ 148 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNuc|Og-294", "decStep|↓ α, after 700 µs", "decNuc|something this lab cannot build", "decNone|1 decay in 700 µs of real time"}},
