  {94, 145, false, 12.499999999999996, {"Bi-212"}, "☢ Plutonium-239: half-life 24,100 years · decays by alpha (α), spontaneous fission 3.1×10⁻¹⁰ %", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:345\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>94</small><b>Pu</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Plutonium</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>actinide</span><span>period 7</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span><span>☢ radioactive: no stable isotope</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> Nuclear reactors, and power for some deep-space probes.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>912 K</b> · 639 °C · 1,182 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>3,503 K</b> · 3,230 °C · 5,846 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (20 °C):</b> 🧊 solid · density 19.7 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:38.98717737923585%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:37.42180987575486%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Plutonium with only one electron left (Pu⁹³⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal plutonium atom, with all 94 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions) (for Plutonium the true lines are shifted and split noticeably, so these are approximate).", "<b>12.5 K</b> · −261 °C · −437 °F  →  Plutonium is <b>🧊 solid</b>", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>36 m/s</b> (≈ 129 km/h). Hotter = faster.", {"decNone|Bi-212: no decays yet. When it decays, each step of the chain is listed here with how long it lived."}},
  {1, 0, true, 293.15000000000003, {}, "● Hydrogen-1: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:110\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>1</small><b>H</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Hydrogen</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>nonmetal</span><span>period 1 · group 1</span><span>☁️ gas at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> The most common element in the universe, and in every water molecule (H₂O). Stars like the Sun run on it.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> <b>14.0 K</b> · −259 °C · −434 °F</div><div><b>Boiling point:</b> <b>20.3 K</b> · −253 °C · −423 °F</div><div><b>At room temperature (68 °F):</b> ☁️ gas · density 0.08 g/L (as a gas)</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:4.8287334433230695%\"></i><i class=\"l\" style=\"width:0.9850432981763836%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>This is hydrogen’s real spectrum:</b> the light it gives off when its electron drops from a higher level to a lower one. The visible lines are the Balmer series (drops to level 2): Hα at 656.47 nm is the red glow of hydrogen in nebulae. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions).", "<b>293 K</b> · 20 °C · 68 °F  →  Hydrogen is <b>☁️ gas</b>", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>6,049 mph</b>. Hotter = faster