  {6, 6, true, 4000, {"Bi-212", "β⁻|3600.5|Po-212", "α|3e-7|Pb-208"}, "● Carbon-12: stable", "<div class=\"ecard\" style=\"--h:110\"><div class=\"etile\"><small>6</small><b>C</b></div><div class=\"ebody\"><div class=\"ename\">Carbon</div><div class=\"echips\"><span>nonmetal</span><span>period 2 · group 14</span><span>🧊 solid at room temperature</span></div><div class=\"euse\"><b>Where you meet it:</b> Pencil “lead” (graphite), diamonds, charcoal, and the backbone of every living thing.</div></div></div><div>Click an element for its most common isotope (longest-lived if none is stable). Dashed outline = no stable isotope.</div>", "<div><b>Melting point:</b> none at normal pressure. It <b>sublimes</b> (solid → gas) at <b>3,900 K</b> · 3,627 °C · 6,560 °F.</div><div><b>At room temperature (68 °F):</b> 🧊 solid · density 2.2 g/cm³</div><div class=\"tbar\"><i class=\"s\" style=\"width:80.6225774829855%\"></i><i class=\"g\" style=\"flex:1\"></i><em style=\"left:22.103921220754778%\" title=\"room temperature\"></em><u class=\"tnow\"></u></div><div class=\"tsc\"><span>0 K</span><span>room</span><span>6000 K</span></div><div class=\"tnote\">Only under enormous pressure does carbon melt.</div><div class=\"tnote\">Values are for a lump of the element at normal air pressure, not for the single atom drawn here.</div>", "<b>The spectrum of Carbon with only one electron left (C⁵⁺)</b>, the atom on screen. It is real: ions like this glow in very hot plasmas and stars. A normal carbon atom, with all 6 electrons, has a very different spectrum, which this lab does not model. Each group is a series of drops to one level; its dashed line is the series limit. ▼ = lines your photon jumps made. Vacuum wavelengths, with the nucleus’s real mass. Line heights are not brightness. Not included: fine structure (relativity splits each line slightly: about 0.005 % for hydrogen, much more for heavy ions).", "<b>4,000 K</b> · 3,727 °C · 6,740 °F  →  Carbon is <b>☁️ gas</b> (it sublimed)", "Typical atom speed here: ≈ <b>6,450 mph</b>. Hotter = faster.", {"decNuc|Bi-212", "decStep|↓ β⁻, after 1 hour", "decNuc|Po-212", "decStep|↓ α, after 3.0×10⁻⁷ s", "decNuc|Pb-208", "decNone|2 decays in 1 hour of real time"}},
